// Package binancefix is a client of Binance Spot's "FIX SBE" order entry
// API: SBE requests and answers, over TLS, on port 9002 of
// fix-oe.binance.com (fix-oe.testnet.binance.vision for the testnet).
//
// FIX SBE is Binance's own protocol, not a FIX Trading Community standard:
// Binance's documentation is its specification, and fix2's strictness rule
// (docs/conformance.md) does not apply to it. The codecs are generated by
// fix2gen sbe from Binance's schema, vendored (internal/fixsbe), and
// exported here through aliases (codecs.go): NewOrderSingle,
// ExecutionReport, Optional, Some, the enumerations' constants.
//
// # Session
//
// A Client runs one connection at a time (Run), reconnecting as told by
// Config.Reconnect. Each connection:
//
//   - logs on with a Logon whose seqNum, 1 (ResetSeqNumFlag: Binance
//     resets on every Logon), and sendingTime are set once and signed
//     with Ed25519 as Binance documents, never stamped again (see
//     signedSendingTime for the textual form of the time, an assumption);
//   - numbers every message it writes, session messages and requests
//     alike, from 2, rolling over from 4,294,967,295 to 0, and stamps its
//     sendingTime as it writes it;
//   - requires Binance's sequence numbers to follow each other exactly,
//     from 1: Binance resends nothing, so a gap or a repeat ends the
//     connection (SequenceError), after our Logout;
//   - sends a Heartbeat after 7/8 of HeartBtInt without writing (Binance
//     never sees HeartBtInt of silence), answers TestRequests, sends a
//     TestRequest after HeartBtInt without reading, and ends the
//     connection when no Heartbeat answers it within another HeartBtInt
//     from when it was written (ErrPeerSilent);
//   - answers Binance's Logout with ours; logs out on Stop, Reconnect or
//     Run's context, waiting Config.LogoutTimeout for Binance's answer;
//   - writes session messages before requests, from a queue of their own:
//     a full request queue never holds back a Heartbeat or a Logout. At
//     most 64 answers to Binance's TestRequests wait to be written: beyond,
//     the writer is stuck, and the connection ends (ErrBacklog).
//
// Without a Logout of ours written, Binance refuses the SenderCompID for
// twice HeartBtInt: Run waits that long before reconnecting. It also makes
// at most 15 attempts in 30 seconds, Binance's limit for order entry, and
// backs off, doubling, while attempts fail. A refused Logon is retried
// after twice HeartBtInt (the refusal may be the SenderCompID's cooldown, or
// a connection limit), Reconnect.MaxRefusals times in a row at most.
//
// # Requests and their receipts
//
// Send encodes a request and queues it on the logged-on connection; it
// never waits, failing with ErrQueueFull when Config.MaxQueued requests
// wait already, and with ErrNotLoggedOn between connections. It returns a
// Receipt, whose State tells where the request stands:
//
//   - Queued, then Written once wholly written;
//   - Answered (or StatusUnknown, Binance's -1007 TIMEOUT on a leg:
//     execution status unknown) once every leg of it is answered (see
//     below);
//   - NeverWritten when its connection ended, or our Logout was queued,
//     before a byte of it was written: Binance never saw it, it is safe to
//     send again;
//   - PossiblyTransmitted when its connection ended after it was written,
//     or partly written, and before an answer: Binance may have executed
//     it. The application reconciles it, as Binance asks for its own
//     timeouts: by the account's ExecutionReports on the next connection
//     (ResponseMode EVERYTHING sends them all), or by querying the order.
//
// The client keeps no store and resends nothing: a request belongs to the
// connection (epoch) it was sent on, and its fate is final once that
// connection ends. OnLogout lists the connection's NeverWritten and
// PossiblyTransmitted requests. A write that fails part way is split at the
// bytes the connection reports written: the requests wholly after them are
// NeverWritten.
//
// A request has legs (Receipt.Legs), each answered on its own: an order,
// a cancellation, an amendment, a list, a mass cancellation, a limits
// query. The request is Answered once every leg is (StatusUnknown if one
// was with -1007), and PossiblyTransmitted if its connection ends first. A
// message answers a leg of a request written and awaiting answers, on the
// request's connection, when it carries the leg's ID and is an answer of
// its kind:
//
//   - an order (NewOrderSingle, an OrderCancelRequestAndNewOrderSingle's
//     new order, a NewOrderList's order), by its ClOrdID: an
//     ExecutionReport or ExecutionReportAck of ExecType NEW (accepted) or
//     REJECTED (refused); for a list's order, its entry in the ListStatus
//     too, its ErrorCode telling;
//   - a cancellation (OrderCancelRequest, an
//     OrderCancelRequestAndNewOrderSingle's CancelClOrdID, which Send then
//     requires): an ExecutionReport CANCELED, or an OrderCancelReject (by
//     the CancelClOrdID or the request's ClOrdID); in STOP_ON_FAILURE mode,
//     a refused cancellation refuses the new order, not attempted; in
//     ALLOW_FAILURE mode, the new order's own answer is awaited;
//   - an amendment (OrderAmendKeepPriorityRequest), whose ClOrdID may be
//     the amended order's: an ExecutionReport REPLACED, or an
//     OrderAmendReject; the order's fills (TRADE) do not answer it;
//   - a list (NewOrderList): its ListStatus, by ClListID, or, ClListID
//     being optional, by an entry naming one of its orders; each entry
//     answers its order with its own error code. A ListStatus never
//     answers a cancellation: an earlier fill's update carries the same
//     IDs;
//   - a mass cancellation: its OrderMassCancelReport; a limits query: its
//     LimitResponse, by ReqID;
//   - any leg: a Reject, by its RefSeqNum, refuses the whole request.
//
// An ExecutionReport of error -1007 makes its leg Unknown whatever its
// ExecType. Later ExecutionReports of an order (fills) reach the Handler
// with no Receipt, as do those of orders sent elsewhere. The IDs of the
// requests awaiting answers are one namespace: Send refuses a request whose
// ID one of them has (ErrDuplicateID). IDs must match Binance's pattern, 1
// to 36 letters, digits, - or _ (ErrInvalidID). With ResponseMode
// EVERYTHING, an order sent elsewhere under the ClOrdID of one of ours
// awaiting its answer would be taken for its answer: keep ClOrdIDs unique
// across the account.
//
// # Handler
//
// The Handler receives, on one goroutine, in order: a connection's
// OnLogon, its messages, its OnLogout, then the next connection's. Its
// methods may call Send, Stop and Reconnect, which never wait, and must
// not wait for Run, nor for a Receipt: the answer may be queued behind the
// call. Messages passed to it are valid during the call only.
// Reading does not wait for the Handler until Config.MaxEvents messages
// wait for it; heartbeats go on regardless. News (Binance's maintenance
// countdown) reaches OnNews: Reconnect(e.Epoch) moves the session to another
// server
// with a clean Logout. LogonEvent.SchemaDeprecated tells that Binance
// deprecated the schema the codecs were generated from.
package binancefix
